Windows host filename compatibility
===================================

check-files.sh host-name builds a Windows fixture using the actual escaped-name
decoder, host charset converter and path resolver. It covers ANSI and UTF-8
trademark names, accents, nested paths, legacy escaped names, normalized-name
collisions and rejection of lossy Unicode-to-MacRoman conversion. It creates
only a dedicated build/host-names.<pid> fixture and removes it afterwards.
The robustness script runs this fixture too.

The optional ROMAW_TEST_HOST_PATH environment variable supplies an existing
escaped host path to resolve without changing it. Set it in PowerShell before
running build/host-names-test.exe from .; custom WSL variables need
WSLENV forwarding to reach Windows executables.

The Windows File Manager interprets raw host names as strict UTF-8 when valid,
otherwise as the active Windows ANSI code page, and converts representable
characters to MacRoman. Escaped bytes retain their existing MacRoman meaning.
Missing-path recovery compares those names against real directory entries;
literal existing paths take precedence and ambiguous normalized matches fail.
This does not rename host files or replace the Windows ANSI filesystem backend.
Unrepresentable names and arbitrary Unicode filesystem access remain unsupported.
An explicit read/open alias for the custom-folder Icon<CR> helper also accepts
the Netatalk/SMB Icon<U+F00D> filename, including its AppleDouble companions.
General Netatalk private-use character mapping remains unsupported.
Recovery must fit within
the original escaped path buffer; it cannot substitute a longer host spelling.

Manual verification used the user's AppleScript trademark-named folder on Y:
and the Installer inside it. Staged ROMAW's FSMakeFSSpec, FSpGetFInfo and
resource-fork open succeeded. This checks lookup and metadata, not Installer
execution or a complete AppleScript installation.

Windows creation uses percent escapes independently of the selected sidecar
layout: colon escapes cannot name Windows folders and can address alternate
streams in files. The fixture now creates and removes names for every nonzero
MacRoman byte except the Macintosh colon separator, verifies decoding, and checks
the Holiday Lemmings 1994 florin name and legacy uppercase percent spelling.
Carriage returns and question marks are escaped on Windows too.

Terminal spaces and periods in every Macintosh path component are escaped as
well. The fixture creates distinct SimCity, SimCity<space> and SimCity<period>
folders, along with all-space/all-period components, verifies round trips, and
checks an encoded parent/child path. The encoder scans each terminal run once.
